      "proof_cmd": "timeit categorise.categorise(s) on cafe_stretched (389 lines) with PYTHONPATH=fd7b64c/src vs T01/src",
      "proof_output": "old categorise_ms=1.45; T01 categorise_ms=1.47"
    },
    {
      "text": "Data, migration, idempotency and rollback: the change is additive. Evidence gains a `ruleset` key, MemoState gains an optional `ruleset`, and Category, PolicyResult and CHECKPOINT_TYPES are unchanged. Old pending reviews resume, as the SQLite proof above shows. Categorisation is a pure function of statement and table, so a rerun gives the same result. Rollback is a reinstall of the previous wheel, which needs no data repair: v1 equals the old code (P7) and no reader requires `ruleset`. PII is unchanged: the table and the evidence add only an id and a hash.",
      "proof_cmd": "git diff fd7b64c..HEAD -- src/cma/models.py src/cma/graph.py | grep CHECKPOINT_TYPES; resume proof above",
      "proof_output": "models.py unchanged; CHECKPOINT_TYPES not in diff; legacy SQLite resume exit=0"
    },
    {
      "text": "Observability and alerting: each memo's evidence records the ruleset id and hash, which shows which table ran. The failure that matters most, a bad or missing table, ends in a non-zero exit (2) with 'Refused.' and the variable named, never a silent default. The repo has no remote alerting by design.",
      "proof_cmd": "pytest tests/test_cli.py -k 'ruleset' -v",
      "proof_output": "test_unknown_cma_ruleset_is_refused_not_blamed_on_the_model PASSED; test_written_evidence_records_the_ruleset PASSED"
    },
    {
      "text": "Docs: README documents CMA_RULESET and the rules file, and renames M16. The categorise.py module docstring describes the data table, duplicate-key refusal and file-bytes hash. The decision itself is recorded in design.md (sub-decision 'where the categorisation rules live').",
      "proof_cmd": "git diff fd7b64c..HEAD -- README.md | grep CMA_RULESET",
      "proof_output": "+# Choose the categorisation table (src/cma/rules/<id>.yaml; default v1)...; +CMA_RULESET=v1 cma run examples/cafe_stretched.csv"
    }
  ],
  "files_changed": [],
  "dispositions": [
    {
      "finding": "major: loader accepts duplicate YAML mapping keys (src/cma/categorise.py:145)",
      "disposition": "not_reproduced",
      "evidence": "At bb0040d a repeated `category` or `rules` key raises RulesetError 'not valid YAML ... duplicate key'. tests pass at HEAD; the safe_load mutant fails 2 tests."
    },
    {
      "finding": "major: legacy resume can fail after the review is committed, leaving no evidence (src/cma/cli.py:213)",
      "disposition": "not_reproduced",
      "evidence": "Real SQLite: a review left pending by fd7b64c and resumed with a damaged table exited 2 with no memo, and resumed with exit 0 once the table was repaired. The unit test passes at HEAD and fails when the pre-decision settle is removed."
    }
  ],
  "notes": "Gaps for the owner, none fixed in code (out of scope): (1) The repo has no CHANGELOG, so there is no release-note line; add one if the project adopts a changelog. (2) MCP compute_metrics/check_policy outputs do not name the ruleset used. The design gives the ruleset id only to evidence and verify (C8), so this needs a design call. (3) Rollback by CMA_RULESET is not yet a flag flip, because v1 is the only table; rollback today means reinstalling the previous wheel. The env flag becomes a real rollback once v2 lands (T-later). (4) The proofs for the real SQLite cross-version resume, the golden rebuild and the wheel install ran by hand in a scratch venv, recorded above. CI covers the wheel load (ci.yml) and runs the SQLite tests because it installs [durable], but CI does not rebuild the golden from fd7b64c; that stays a manual, documented step (make_p7_baseline.py docstring). (5) The golden's _about text says the tools were in the run directory; the docstring in tests/golden/p7_tools explains this. No checklist item needed a new dependency or service."
}

